---
import ArticleClosingNote from '@/components/articles/ArticleClosingNote.astro';
import ArticleImage from '@/components/ui/ArticleImage.astro';
import ArticlePinSaveSection from '@/components/articles/ArticlePinSaveSection.astro';
import ArticleShareBar from '@/components/articles/ArticleShareBar.astro';
import hozomonGate from '@/assets/articles/tokyo/senso-ji-hozomon-gate-asakusa.webp';
import templeInterior from '@/assets/articles/tokyo/senso-ji-temple-interior-details.webp';
import fiveStoryPagoda from '@/assets/articles/tokyo/senso-ji-five-story-pagoda-asakusa.webp';
import meijiSakeBarrels1 from '@/assets/articles/tokyo/sake-barrels-meiji-jingu-shrine-tokyo.webp';
import meijiToriiGate from '@/assets/articles/tokyo/meiji-jingu-torii-gate-tokyo.webp';
import meijiSakeBarrels2 from '@/assets/articles/tokyo/meiji-jingu-sake-barrels-tokyo.webp';
import hieToriiStairs from '@/assets/articles/tokyo/hie-shrine-torii-stairs-tokyo.webp';
import hieToriiGates from '@/assets/articles/tokyo/walking-through-hie-shrine-torii-gates-tokyo.webp';
import gotokujiTemple from '@/assets/articles/tokyo/gotokuji-temple-tokyo.webp';
import gotokujiCats from '@/assets/articles/tokyo/gotokuji-temple-lucky-cat-statues.webp';
import tokyoTempleGuidePin from '@/assets/articles/tokyo/tokyo-temple-guide-pin.png';

const prose = 'article-prose max-w-3xl text-black/80 [&_p]:mb-5 [&_p:last-child]:mb-0';
---

<section id="section-0" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>1. Sensō-ji Temple – Asakusa</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Sensō-ji is one of the most iconic temples in Tokyo and one of the best places to experience the city’s traditional side. The visit usually begins at Kaminarimon Gate, with its huge red lantern, before continuing along Nakamise-dori, a historic shopping street lined with stalls selling souvenirs, sweets, and traditional snacks. Inside the temple grounds, you’ll also find Hōzōmon Gate, the main hall, and the five-storied pagoda, making Sensō-ji much more than just a quick photo stop.</p>
    <div class="my-5 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      <ArticleImage
        src={hozomonGate}
        alt="Hozomon Gate at Sensō-ji Temple in Asakusa, Tokyo"
        caption="Hozomon Gate"
        figureClass="m-0"
        imgClass="aspect-[3/4] object-cover"
      />
      <ArticleImage
        src={templeInterior}
        alt="Interior details at Sensō-ji Temple in Asakusa, Tokyo"
        caption="Temple interior"
        figureClass="m-0"
        imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover"
      />
      <ArticleImage
        src={fiveStoryPagoda}
        alt="Five-story pagoda at Sensō-ji Temple in Asakusa, Tokyo"
        caption="Five-story pagoda"
        figureClass="m-0"
        imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover"
      />
    </div>
    <p><span class="font-medium text-black">Location:</span> Asakusa</p>
    <p><span class="font-medium text-black">Best for:</span> traditional Tokyo atmosphere, temple grounds, souvenir streets, and evening photos</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-1" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>2. Meiji Jingu – Harajuku</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Meiji Jingu is one of the most peaceful places you can visit in central Tokyo. It sits inside a large forested area between Harajuku and Shibuya, and the walk toward the shrine feels like a complete change of pace from the busy streets nearby.</p>
    <p>What makes Meiji Jingu special is not only the shrine itself, but the transition. One moment you are near Harajuku, and the next you are walking under a massive torii gate, surrounded by tall trees and quiet forest paths. It is one of the best places in Tokyo to experience a Shinto shrine without leaving the city center.</p>
    
    <div class="my-5 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <ArticleImage
        src={meijiSakeBarrels1}
        alt="Sake barrels at Meiji Jingu Shrine in Tokyo"
        caption="Sake barrels"
        figureClass="m-0 sm:col-span-2"
        imgClass="aspect-[5/4] object-cover"
      />
      <ArticleImage
        src={meijiToriiGate}
        alt="Torii gate at Meiji Jingu Shrine in Tokyo"
        caption="Torii gate"
        figureClass="m-0"
        imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover"
      />
      <ArticleImage
        src={meijiSakeBarrels2}
        alt="Sake barrels display at Meiji Jingu Shrine in Tokyo"
        caption="Sake barrels display"
        figureClass="m-0"
        imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover"
      />
    </div>
    
    <p><span class="font-medium text-black">Location:</span> Harajuku / Yoyogi</p>
    <p><span class="font-medium text-black">Best for:</span> forest paths, peaceful atmosphere, shrine rituals, and pairing with Harajuku or Yoyogi Park</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-2" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>3. Hie Shrine – Akasaka</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Hie Shrine feels like a hidden gem, even though it is located right in central Tokyo. It is not as famous as Sensō-ji or Meiji Jingu, but that is exactly what makes it worth adding to your itinerary. The highlight here is the tunnel of red torii gates, which feels like a smaller and quieter version of the torii gate paths many people associate with Kyoto. The shrine is also connected to Sanno Matsuri, one of Tokyo’s major traditional festivals, giving it more meaning than just being a pretty photo stop.</p>
    <div class="my-5 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <ArticleImage
        src={hieToriiStairs}
        alt="Red torii gate stairs at Hie Shrine in Akasaka, Tokyo"
        caption="Torii stairs at Hie Shrine"
        figureClass="m-0"
        imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover"
      />
      <ArticleImage
        src={hieToriiGates}
        alt="Walking through the red torii tunnel at Hie Shrine in Akasaka, Tokyo"
        caption="Walking through the torii gates"
        figureClass="m-0"
        imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover"
      />
    </div>
    <p><span class="font-medium text-black">Location:</span> Akasaka / Nagatachō</p>
    <p><span class="font-medium text-black">Best for:</span> red torii gates, a quieter shrine experience, and a beautiful hidden stop in central Tokyo</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-3" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>4. Gotokuji Temple – Setagaya</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>If you love cats, unique temples, or photo-worthy places, Gotokuji Temple is worth adding to your Tokyo itinerary. It is one of the cutest temples in Tokyo and is best known for its many maneki-neko, or lucky cat figures, which make the temple instantly recognizable.</p>
    <p>The temple is located in Setagaya, a quieter residential area away from Tokyo’s busiest neighborhoods. From Shinjuku Station, you can take the Odakyu Line to Gotokuji Station and walk from there, so plan for around 25–30 minutes in total depending on connections.</p>
    <div class="my-5 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <ArticleImage
        src={gotokujiCats}
        alt="Rows of maneki-neko lucky cat statues at Gotokuji Temple in Setagaya, Tokyo"
        caption="Lucky cat statues"
        figureClass="m-0"
        imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover"
      />
      <ArticleImage
        src={gotokujiTemple}
        alt="Gotokuji Temple grounds in Setagaya, Tokyo"
        caption="Gotokuji Temple"
        figureClass="m-0"
        imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover"
      />
    </div>
    <p><span class="font-medium text-black">Location:</span> Setagaya</p>
    <p><span class="font-medium text-black">Best for:</span> lucky cats, cute photos, and a quieter local neighborhood feel</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-4" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class={prose}>
    <ArticleClosingNote label="Before you go">
      <p>Don’t forget that you can find all the temples and shrines from this guide pinned on my Ultimate Tokyo Map.</p>
      <p>One small thing to keep in mind: temples and shrines in Japan are not just sightseeing spots, but active places of worship. Be respectful, follow any signs about photography, and keep the atmosphere peaceful while you visit.</p>
      <p>👉 <a href="/articles/tokyo-5-day-itinerary/" class="text-[#69746E] underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-[#4a534f]">Read my 5-day Tokyo itinerary and get the Ultimate Tokyo Map here</a>.</p>
    </ArticleClosingNote>

    <ArticlePinSaveSection>
      <ArticleImage
        src={tokyoTempleGuidePin}
        alt="Tokyo temple guide — best shrines and temples to visit in Tokyo"
        figureClass="m-0"
        pinOnHover
      />

      <ArticleShareBar
        title="Best Temples and Shrines in Tokyo to Add to Your Itinerary"
        pinImage={tokyoTempleGuidePin}
        class="mt-6 md:mt-8"
      />
    </ArticlePinSaveSection>
  </div>
</section>
